      "html": "<ul><li>Scientific name: Hypostomus sculpodon Armbruster, 2003</li><li>IUCN Red List category: Not Evaluated</li><li>Breeding: unreported</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1332",
      "html": "<p>South America: Upper Rio Negro and upper Rio Orinoco basins in Venezuela. Type locality: Piedra Carguana, Depto. Río Negro, Río Negro drainage, Río Casiquiare, Amazonas, Venezuela.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1332",
      "html": "<p>Distinguished from all other members of the Hypostomus cochliodon group except H. hemicochliodon by having a buccal papilla and intermediately developed teeth (juveniles of H. cochliodon and H. taphorni occasionally also have intermediate teeth). It differs from H. hemicochliodon by having brownish-red coloration with widely spaced spots versus dark brown with closely spaced spots, and by having a smaller dorsal-anal length to anal-fin length ratio. The specific epithet refers to the ability of the species to chisel wood with its teeth.</p>"
    },
